/** @typedef {{dur:number,srate?:number,chans?:number}} SourceInfo */
/** @typedef {SourceInfo & {in2?:SourceInfo}} SrcContext */
/** @typedef {{name:string,label:string,min:number,max:number,default:number|string,step?:number,help?:string,hardMin?:number,hardMax?:number,choices?:[string,string][],minOf?:(values:Record<string,number|string>)=>number,maxOf?:(values:Record<string,number|string>)=>number,srcMin?:(src:SrcContext,values:Record<string,number|string>)=>number,srcMax?:(src:SrcContext,values:Record<string,number|string>)=>number,srcHardMin?:(src:SrcContext,values:Record<string,number|string>)=>number,srcHardMax?:(src:SrcContext,values:Record<string,number|string>)=>number,srcDefault?:(src:SrcContext)=>number}} Param */
// `help` is a short, plain-language description of what the parameter does (shown
// in a consuming app's curated ? panel). Keep it one or two sentences, no CDP jargon.
//
// `min`/`max` are a *curated* span — the musically useful part of what the
// program accepts, sized so a slider's travel is worth having. They are usually
// narrower than CDP's own limits, and deliberately so: audit-param-ranges.mjs
// treats a range wider than the engine's as an error and a narrower one as an
// advisory. `hardMin`/`hardMax` optionally record the engine's real limit (from
// the program's own `ap->lo[]`/`ap->hi[]` table, or its manpage) so a consumer
// can offer to unlock the full span; where they are absent, an unlocking UI just
// stops clamping and lets CDP reject what it will not take.
//
// `srcMin`/`srcMax`/`srcDefault` cover the parameters whose useful span is a
// function of the *input sound* rather than a constant. That is not a fringe
// case: CDP fills its own `ap->lo[]`/`ap->hi[]` validation table from the
// infile's duration at startup (dev/cdp2k/mainfuncs.c
// `setup_param_ranges_and_defaults`), which is why `extend zigzag` insists its
// output be longer than its input. `srcHardMin`/`srcHardMax` are the distinct
// source-dependent engine limits that must survive unlocking a curated span.
// The rule for choosing: a source bound that folds in taste (`max(8, dur * 8)`
// as a sensible output ceiling) is `srcMin`/`srcMax`; one that is purely the
// engine's goes in `srcHardMin`/`srcHardMax` as well (mchshred's scatter cap),
// or unlocking to a static `hardMin`/`hardMax` recorded on the same side would
// reopen it — catalog-schema.mjs checks for that. See paramRange /
// paramDefaultFor / defaultsFor below.
//
// A source can also be too short for an effect *at any settings* — zigzag's
// window must hold a minimum zig in both directions, which no combination
// achieves below 0.1 s. Such an entry declares `minSrcDur` (seconds), and
// consumers reject shorter sources before resolving ranges: paramRange cannot
// express "no range" (an over-constrained span collapses to a single value,
// which for a too-short source would look usable while violating the very
// constraint it encodes).
//
// A few programs also have a *longest* workable source, for the mirror-image
// reason: a source-relative floor meets a fixed engine ceiling (shrink's gap
// must be at least the source duration but never more than 60 s; sfecho's
// delay at least the source but at most an hour). Such an entry declares
// `maxSrcDur` (seconds), with the same contract: reject longer sources up
// front rather than resolving ranges against them.

